#              usable credential.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Without it, a leased session is fixed to the account it got
# at startup. Hit that account's rate limit and the session STOPS — the pool
# reroutes the NEXT process, not this one. Same for token expiry: the server
# keeps its copy fresh, but nothing here re-fetches it.
#
# HUNK 1 — services/compact/precomputedCompact.js, in withRetryGenerator.
#   On a 429, report the limit to claudiverse, take the account it hands back,
#   invalidate the token cache and `continue`. A switch costs one retry instead
#   of a failed turn.
#
#   🔴 POSITION IS LOAD-BEARING: AT THE TOP OF THE CATCH, NOT NEAR THE BOTTOM.
#   v1 of this patch inserted at :116721, just before `let cn = bTn(Ke)`, which
#   is where the 2.1.238 patch sat. It NEVER FIRED. Cause: TWENTY early exits
#   sit between the catch and that point, and one of them claims 429s first —
#     let je = Mr() ? d.fastMode && !Sse() : !1;                  (:116385)
#     if (je && !dR() && Ke instanceof Lt && (Ke.status === 429 || AD(Ke))) {
#         ... d.fastMode = !1; continue;                          (:116555)
#   With FAST MODE ON a rate limit disables fast mode and retries, consuming the
#   error 166 lines before our hook. MEASURED: the sidecar log showed only
#   `leased Anthropic account` and NOT ONE `Switched`, `no account available`,
#   or even the under-threshold line — proof the function was never CALLED, as
#   distinct from returning the wrong answer.
#   ⚠️ I verified the insertion point was correct and never checked whether
#   anything UPSTREAM of it claims a 429. A correct anchor in the wrong position
#   applies cleanly, builds, and does nothing.
#
#   Now inserted immediately after `let ct = await r.onError?.(Ke)` — after the
#   three non-retryable rethrows (O_, Xl, DLP) and after logging and the
#   onError callback, but BEFORE every handler that can continue. The pool gets
#   first refusal; when it cannot help it returns switched:false and every
#   existing handler runs exactly as before. That is also better behaviour on
#   the merits: switching to a healthy account beats degrading fast mode on an
#   exhausted one. This is the 2.1.263 successor to the 2.1.238 site the old
#   004-multi-account patch hooked (Ny6 -> withRetryGenerator, P -> Ke,
#   cq -> Lt, vw3 -> Ako, dIK -> bTn).
#
#   🔑 Hw() IS LOAD-BEARING. It is the cache invalidator on this version
#   (coreSchemas.js:35005 -> iH() -> Use()). Swapping the env var WITHOUT it
#   changes nothing: the process keeps serving the old token from the memo and
#   the failover reports success while doing nothing. An earlier attempt put the
#   clear inside SidecarClient, where the binding does not exist — behind a
#   `typeof` guard, so it threw nothing, logged nothing, and never ran.
#
#   🔴 WHEN NO ACCOUNT IS AVAILABLE WE DO NOTHING AND FALL THROUGH, deliberately.
#   Upstream already owns rate-limit backoff, the resets-at message and
#   auto-continue. Operator: "it should work like claude usually does, it should
#   just stick with the first refreshing one and pause until its available".
#   Substituting our own waiting would replace working behaviour with a worse
#   copy. The account is still cooled SERVER-side so new sessions route around
#   it; this one keeps its token, which stays valid because a rate limit is not
#   an auth failure.
#
# HUNK 2 — main.js, in runOnboarding (ja), the interactive-only path.
#   Registers our pool lease as upstream's SDK OAuth refresh callback. Upstream
#   fires it from recoverFromOAuth401 EXACTLY when there is no local refresh
#   token, which is precisely our state (CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN implies
#   refreshToken:null), then installs the result and clears its own caches. So
#   the 401/expiry path needs no logic here at all — only registration.
#   cli/print.js already registers one for print/SDK mode; this covers the REPL.
#
#   ⚠️ GATED ON ACTUALLY LEASING, and that is not cosmetic. The retry loop reads
#   kxe() at precomputedCompact.js:116420: with a callback registered, a refresh
#   that returns the SAME token fails fast instead of retrying N times. Correct
#   for us — the server is the authority, so retrying cannot help — but wrong to
#   impose on a stock session that never leased. Hence the CLAUDIVERSE_TOKEN and
#   CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN guards.
#
# ANCHOR: hunk 1 lands inside `async function* withRetryGenerator(...)` — an
#   ANCHORED name, so even git's @@ context hint is version-stable. The added
#   lines reference Lt / Ako / Mot / Hw, which are minified; all four are
#   verified in scope in that file (118 / 2 / 4 / 18 refs). They sit on added
#   lines, so drift fails at RUNTIME rather than at apply time — accepted
#   because no anchored equivalent exists for them, and the surrounding context
#   lines still fail LOUDLY on structural drift.
#
# ⚠️ NOT EXERCISED: neither path has been triggered against a real 429 or a real
#   expiry. Applying, parsing and bundling are VERIFIED; the behaviour is
#   BELIEVED. Forcing a 429 needs an account actually at its limit.
#
